What <parameter> in testng.xml actually does

In TestNG, parameters in testng.xml are configuration inputs, not test data.

They are injected into test methods using @Parameters.

📌 Why are parameters written ABOVE <test>?

Example:

<suite name="Suite">

    <parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>
    <parameter name="env" value="qa"/>

    <test name="Login Tests">
        <classes>
            <class name="tests.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

</suite>
🧠 Reason (important concept)

Because TestNG has a hierarchy:

suite level  →  test level  →  class level  → method level

So:

<suite> parameters = global (available everywhere)
<test> parameters = only inside that test tag
<class> parameters = only for that class (rarely used)
📌 Do suite parameters go to ALL tests?
✅ YES — IF you design it that way
Suite-level parameter:
<parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>

👉 Available to:

ALL <test> blocks
ALL classes
ALL methods (if used with @Parameters)
📌 But IMPORTANT: It does NOT automatically inject data

TestNG will NOT magically give values.

You must explicitly request it:

✔️ Example test method
@Test
@Parameters({"browser", "env"})
public void loginTest(String browser, String env) {

    System.out.println(browser);
    System.out.println(env);
}
🧠 What actually happens internally

When TestNG runs:

Reads testng.xml
Finds parameters at suite/test/class level
Matches them with @Parameters
Injects values into methods
📌 Will suite parameters go to ALL test methods?
✔️ YES — BUT only if:
Method declares them using @Parameters
❌ NO — if method does not declare them

Example:

@Test
public void loginTest() {
    System.out.println("No parameters here");
}

👉 This method will NOT receive anything.

📌 Suite vs Test vs Class parameters (VERY IMPORTANT)
1. Suite level (global)
<suite>
    <parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>
</suite>

👉 Available everywhere in suite

2. Test level
<test name="Login">
    <parameter name="browser" value="firefox"/>
</test>

👉 Overrides suite parameter for this test only

3. Class level (rare use)
<class name="tests.LoginTest">
    <parameter name="browser" value="edge"/>
</class>

👉 Overrides test/suite

🧠 Priority order (VERY IMPORTANT)

If same parameter exists:

class > test > suite

So:

suite browser = chrome
test browser = firefox

👉 Result = firefox (test level wins)

📌 Do parameters go to ALL test files?
✔️ YES — if:
They are at suite level
And the class uses @Parameters

Example:

Works for ALL classes:
<suite>
    <parameter name="env" value="qa"/>
</suite>
Used in multiple test files:
@Parameters("env")

GOAL

You want:

mvn test -DsuiteXmlFile=testng.xml -Dbrowser=chrome -Denv=qa

And use:

browser
env

inside TestNG tests.

🧾 1. testng.xml (NO hardcoding of values)

👉 We define parameters, but we also allow CLI override

<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM "https://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd">

<suite name="Automation Suite">

    <!-- default values (fallback if CLI not passed) -->
    <parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>
    <parameter name="env" value="qa"/>

    <test name="Login Tests">

        <classes>
            <class name="tests.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>

    </test>

</suite>
